INT. FUNERAL HOME - VIEWING ROOM B - DAY

Velvet drapes. A casket at the far end, closed, spray of lilies. Folding chairs facing a mahogany table, court reporter's stenotype ticking beside it.

ELOISE FARNSWORTH, 70s, black gloves, rosary wrapped around one hand, sits very straight. On her left, CAMILA BRANDAO, 40s, her attorney, legal pad flat. Across the table, DOROTHEA KWAN, 50s, opposing counsel, reading glasses on a chain.

KWAN
Mrs. Farnsworth, when did you last see your husband alive?

ELOISE
Tuesday.

KWAN
And the will?

Eloise's rosary clicks once.

ELOISE
What about it?

KWAN
Did she witness it?

BRANDAO
Objection. Who's "she"?

KWAN
The nurse.

ELOISE
Mrs. Okafor.

KWAN
She told us she wasn't present.

Eloise stares at the casket. Her fingers work the beads, one, two, three.

BRANDAO
Let the record show my client has not been served that statement.

KWAN
It's in your folder.

Brandao flips pages. Stops. Her thumb presses a corner of paper until it whitens.

ELOISE
I'd like some water.

Kwan nods to the reporter. A pitcher is brought. Eloise's glove shakes as she lifts the cup, then stills.
